PLACES=[
N('solitude-house-union-forge','Solitude House and the Union Forge site','High Bridge','njc','industry','standing',40.67105,-74.8863,'site','view','walk','','1742–1972',
  'The stone house began as an early 1700s farmhouse and became the home of the managers of the Union Iron Works, founded here in 1742 by William Allen and Joseph Turner of Philadelphia; five generations of the Taylor family, who ran the works, lived in it. The markers say the forge beside it made cannonballs for Washington\'s army, and that Lake Solitude was dammed on the South Branch of the Raritan in 1858 to power the works. The borough took the house in 2000 and the Union Forge Heritage Association ran it as a museum from 2002 until the borough closed that in October 2012.',
  'The house, its stone annex and the dam of Lake Solitude with its 175-foot spillway (once called Taylor Falls), all on River Road. Markers for the house, the forge and the lake stand together there. What, if anything, shows of the forge itself was not checked. Whether the house is open now was not checked.',
  'Where cannonballs for the Revolution were forged, and the house the ironmasters lived in.',
  'Owned by the Borough of High Bridge. The museum that opened the house closed in 2012 and no current opening was found, so treat the house as view-only from River Road. Parking at the Lake Solitude lot on River Road (mapped as borough open space). The Taylor Steelworkers Historic Greenway, a 7-mile path laid out by the heritage association, links the house with the TISCO complex. Borough hours not checked: its parks page refused the tools.',
  [HM(37036,'Solitude House'),HM(37043,'Union Forge'),HM(37041,'Lake Solitude and Taylor Falls'),HM(73998,'Solitude'),UFHA_HIST,GREENWAY],conf='mixed',
  th=[40.67203,-74.88574],thn='Lake Solitude parking, River Road'),
N('tisco-complex-high-bridge','TISCO complex (Taylor Iron and Steel office and Shop E)','High Bridge','njc','industry','standing',40.66915,-74.8886,'site','view','walk','','1742–1972',
  'A 28-acre piece of the old Taylor-Wharton Iron and Steel works, which grew out of the Union Iron Works of 1742 and stopped work in 1972. The heritage association\'s marker calls Taylor-Wharton the country\'s oldest continuously operating foundry; it says the stone office building dates to about 1725, held the company offices from the start to 1972 and also served as company store and infirmary. Shop E was an early industrial building used as a machine shop and for grinding forged metal.',
  'Two buildings: the stone TISCO office and the long Shop E. The marker stands behind Union Forge Park on Washington Avenue. Their present condition was not checked.',
  '',
  'The marker says the property is now permanent borough open space; the buildings are not open. Park at Union Forge Park on Washington Avenue. Borough hours not checked.',
  [HM(37048,'The TISCO Complex'),HM(36997,'T.I.S.C.O. Complex'),HM(5043,'Taylor Iron and Steel Company'),GREENWAY],conf='mixed',
  th=[40.66625,-74.89295],thn='Union Forge Park, Washington Avenue'),
N('high-bridge-railroad-arches','Central Railroad of New Jersey embankment and arches','High Bridge','njc','rail','standing',40.66482,-74.8946,'site','view','drive','','1859–1865',
  'The Central Railroad of New Jersey crossed the valley here first on a high bridge, which gave the borough its name, and between 1859 and 1865 replaced it with a 112-foot earth embankment carried over the stream on a double stone arch.',
  'The double arch where Arch Street passes under the embankment.',
  'The bridge a town is named for, buried in its own embankment.',
  'Public street. Whether trains still use the embankment was not checked; stay off it. Parking at Union Forge Park on Washington Avenue.',
  [HM(5040,'Railroad Bridge and Arches')],conf='mixed',
  th=[40.66625,-74.89295],thn='Union Forge Park, Washington Avenue'),
N('ken-lockwood-gorge-bridge','Ken Lockwood Gorge Bridge and the 1885 train wreck','Lebanon Township','njc','rail','standing',40.69725,-74.87262,'site','public','walk','','1885 wreck',
  'The High Bridge Branch of the Central Railroad of New Jersey, finished in 1875, carried iron ore from the Morris County mines down to the forges at High Bridge. On April 18, 1885 the middle and southern spans of its wooden Howe truss bridge over the gorge fell as a 46-car ore train started across; the locomotive Columbia dropped onto the hillside and the cars into the South Branch of the Raritan, and the crew jumped clear. TrailLink says the present steel bridge was built in 1930 and dates the wreck to 1886; the county marker and Wikipedia give 1885. The branch was dropped in 1976 and is now the Columbia Trail.',
  'The steel trestle about 80 feet above the river, crossed on the trail; the wreck marker stands at its south end.',
  'The 1885 ore train that fell into the Raritan.',
  'Columbia Trail, run by Hunterdon County Parks on a gas-company right-of-way; the gorge below is the Ken Lockwood Gorge Wildlife Management Area (state Division of Fish and Wildlife; hunting and trout fishing). Park at the Columbia Trail lot on Main Street in High Bridge and walk about 2.7 miles north (distance from NJ Hiking). Morris County gives the trail sunrise to sunset; Hunterdon County\'s own hours not checked.',
  [HM(21806,'The Gorge Bridge Train Wreck'),HM(5292,'High Bridge and Longwood Valley Branch'),COLUMBIA,W('Ken Lockwood Gorge'),
   L('TrailLink: Taylor SteelWorkers Historical Greenway guide (PDF, 2025)','https://www.traillink.com/gdbkpdf/trail/6349541/taylor-steelworkers-historical-greenway-63893574705805-2025.pdf'),
   L('NJ Hiking: Columbia Trail','https://www.njhiking.com/columbia-trail/'),NYNJTC_KLG,MORRIS_CT],conf='mixed',
  th=[40.66966,-74.89762],thn='Columbia Trail lot, Main Street, High Bridge'),
N('red-mill-clinton','Red Mill and Mulligan quarry lime kilns','Clinton','njc','industry','standing',40.63611,-74.91278,'exact','open','drive','','1810–1928',
  'Ralph Hunt built the first part of the Red Mill on the South Branch of the Raritan about 1810 to process wool; over its working life it also ground grain, plaster and graphite. Local people bought it in 1960, and in 1964 the adjoining M. C. Mulligan & Sons limestone quarry was added; they became the Clinton Historical Museum, now the Red Mill Museum Village. At the quarry, stone was crushed for roads and burned in kilns into quicklime for farm fields.',
  'The four-storey red mill by the falls, and in the quarry the lime kilns, crusher and screen house, with other old buildings on the museum grounds.',
  'A wool mill, a quarry and its lime kilns in one museum.',
  'Red Mill Museum Village, 56 Main Street; Wikipedia says the mill and quarry were given to the Clinton Historical Museum, which runs the site (ownership not otherwise checked). Its visit page (read November 17, 2026) gives Wednesday to Friday 10 to 4, Saturday and Sunday 11 to 5; adults $12. Limited visitor parking in front of the mill; the museum lists other lots nearby.',
  [W('Red Mill (Clinton, New Jersey)'),L('Red Mill Museum Village: visitor information','https://theredmill.org/visit/'),HM(140749,'The Lime Kilns'),HM(63099,'Mulligan Quarry')],hours='Wednesday to Friday 10 to 4; Saturday and Sunday 11 to 5'),
N('stone-mill-clinton','Dunham\'s Mill (the Stone Mill, now the Hunterdon Art Museum)','Clinton','njc','industry','standing',40.63657,-74.91187,'exact','open','drive','','1951 (milling ended)',
  'The stone grist mill across the river from the Red Mill. Sources disagree on its age: the marker on Lower Center Street says it was built in 1837 on a site milled since 1751, while Wikipedia says Daniel Hunt built the stone mill in 1763. Both agree it is now the art museum; the marker says milling went on until 1951.',
  'The stone mill on the east bank of the South Branch, facing the Red Mill across the falls.',
  '',
  'Hunterdon Art Museum, 7 Lower Center Street, which uses the mill building (who owns it was not checked). Its visit page (read November 17, 2026) gives Wednesday to Sunday 11 to 5, closed Monday and Tuesday; adults $7.',
  [HM(57080,'Dunham\'s Mill'),W('Red Mill (Clinton, New Jersey)'),L('Hunterdon Art Museum: plan your visit','https://www.hunterdonartmuseum.org/visit/')],conf='mixed',hours='Wednesday to Sunday 11 to 5; closed Monday and Tuesday'),
N('prallsville-mills','Prallsville Mills','Stockton','njc','industry','standing',40.40952,-74.98585,'exact','public','drive','canal','1794–1950s',
  'A mill village where Wickecheoke Creek meets the Delaware. John Prall Jr. bought the mill seat in 1794, built a stone grist mill and a linseed oil mill and ran a saw mill, quarries and a store. In 1874 a fire that began in the covered railroad bridge behind the mill burned it; within three years a new mill went up on the old foundation and walls. The Delaware and Raritan feeder canal (1834) and the Belvidere-Delaware Railroad pass through the site. The state bought it in 1973, and the Delaware River Mill Society has leased and restored it since 1976.',
  'The 1877 grist mill, the linseed oil mill, the saw mill, the grain silo of about 1900 and other buildings, ten in all, beside the canal feeder and its spillway.',
  'A whole mill village with a canal running through it.',
  'Part of Delaware and Raritan Canal State Park, run by the Delaware River Mill Society, 33 Risler Street. The State Park Service page (read only as quoted in a web search result) gives the grounds sunrise to sunset, no entrance fee, building tours as posted on the Mill Society\'s site. Towpath parking on Risler Street.',
  [L('Delaware River Mill Society: history','https://prallsvillemills.org/history/'),HM(21268,'Grist Mill'),HM(21269,'Mill Power System'),
   L('NJ State Park Service: Prallsville Mills','https://nj.gov/dep/parksandforests/historic/prallsvillemills.html'),
   L('New Jersey Historic Trust: Prallsville Mill saw mill','https://www.nj.gov/dca/njht/funded/sitedetails/prallsville_mill_saw_mill.shtml'),W('Prallsville, New Jersey')],
  th=[40.40948,-74.98597],thn='D&R Canal towpath parking, Risler Street'),
N('green-sergeants-covered-bridge','Green Sergeant\'s Covered Bridge','Delaware Township','njc','landmark','standing',40.44417,-74.96639,'exact','public','drive','','1872',
  'The last historic covered bridge in New Jersey, built in 1872 over Wickecheoke Creek to a design by Charles Ogden Holcombe of Lambertville, on abutments the marker dates to colonial times. Taken down in 1960 to make way for a modern span, it was rebuilt from its own timbers in 1961 after a public outcry. Trucks have struck it several times since, most recently in 2013, Wikipedia says.',
  'The timber bridge carrying the westbound lane of Rosemont-Ringoes Road (County Route 604), with a modern bridge beside it.',
  'New Jersey\'s last covered bridge, saved from the scrapheap by its neighbours.',
  'Public road (Hunterdon County Route 604); the federal land map puts the bridge inside the Wickecheoke Creek Preserve. No parking lot is mapped beside it; where to pull off safely was not checked. The nearest mapped lot is the Wickecheoke Creek Preserve lot on Upper Creek Road, about a mile north.',
  [W('Green Sergeant\'s Covered Bridge'),HM(16828,'Green Sergeant\'s Bridge')]),
N('lambertville-outlet-lock','Lambertville outlet lock','Lambertville','njc','canal','traces',40.35875,-74.94425,'site','public','walk','canal','1848–1912',
  'Built in 1848 so that coal boats from Pennsylvania\'s Delaware Canal could cross the river and enter the Delaware and Raritan feeder canal. Boats were hooked to a cable strung across the Delaware at New Hope and let the current carry them over; the mules walked across the road bridge and through town. The Lambertville Beacon counted about 31 boats a day in 1868. The lock was abandoned in 1912.',
  'The marker stands a few hundred feet down the towpath from the west end of Mount Hope Street, downslope from the towpath, at the lock site; how much of the lock is left was not checked, so look there. The canal widens nearby where boats turned into the lock.',
  'Coal boats ferried across the Delaware on a cable.',
  'Delaware and Raritan Canal State Park towpath: open sunrise to sunset, dogs on a leash (dandrcanal.org). Reach it from the west end of Mount Hope Street. No public lot was identified: park on Lambertville streets (not checked).',
  [HM(100644,'Lambertville Outlet Lock'),L('dandrcanal.org: canal locks','https://dandrcanal.org/history/locks'),DRC_INFO],conf='mixed'),
N('lumberville-raven-rock-bridge','Lumberville–Raven Rock pedestrian bridge','Delaware Township','njc','landmark','standing',40.40797,-75.03715,'exact','public','walk','','1947',
  'A footbridge hung from cables in several spans across the Delaware between Bulls Island and Lumberville, Pennsylvania. The toll bridge commission\'s marker says John A. Roebling\'s Sons Co. of Trenton, builders of the Brooklyn Bridge, built it in 1947 to replace a road bridge of four wooden covered spans and one steel span, which had been a shareholders\' toll bridge for 75 years until 1932. It was rehabilitated in 2013.',
  'Walk across the cable spans; the feeder canal begins at Bulls Island on the New Jersey end.',
  '',
  'The New Jersey end is Bulls Island Recreation Area, Delaware and Raritan Canal State Park (state); the marker on the bridge was put up by the Delaware River Joint Toll Bridge Commission, but who owns the bridge was not checked. Canal park open sunrise to sunset, dogs on a leash (dandrcanal.org); the State Park Service page (read only as quoted in a web search result) describes the bridge and the park office. Park at the D&R Canal trail lot on Route 29 (Daniel Bray Highway).',
  [HM(68318,'Lumberville - Ravenrock Toll-Supported Pedestrian Bridge'),W('Bulls Island Recreation Area'),
   L('NJ State Park Service: Bulls Island Recreation Area','https://nj.gov/dep/parksandforests/parks/bullsislandrecreationarea.html'),DRC_INFO],
  th=[40.41095,-75.0344],thn='D&R Canal trail parking, Route 29, Raven Rock'),
N('hunterdon-county-courthouse','Hunterdon County Courthouse (the Hauptmann trial)','Flemington','njc','civic','standing',40.5104,-74.8593,'exact','view','drive','','1828',
  'Built in 1828 after the first courthouse burned, with stone from the old building used for the jail behind it. In 1935 Bruno Hauptmann was tried here for the kidnapping and murder of Charles Lindbergh\'s infant son, the "Trial of the Century", and sentenced to death. The jail closed in 1985 and the courts moved to a new justice center in 1996.',
  'The courthouse on Main Street, with the stone jail behind it.',
  'The room where the Lindbergh case was tried.',
  'County-owned. View from Main Street; whether the building is open to visitors was not checked. Town parking nearby.',
  [W('Hunterdon County Courthouse')]),
N('locktown-baptist-church','Locktown Baptist Church','Delaware Township','njc','worship','standing',40.48494,-74.97058,'exact','view','drive','','1819',
  'A two-storey fieldstone church built in 1819 by the Kingwood Baptist congregation, organized in 1745, as its "Lower Church", south of Wickecheoke Creek. Its first pastor, David Bateman, who died in 1832, is buried under the floor.',
  'The plain stone meeting house on Locktown-Sergeantsville Road.',
  '',
  'The federal land map shows the church lot as open space named Locktown Stone Church; who owns it, and whether it opens, was not checked. View from Locktown-Sergeantsville Road; no parking is mapped.',
  [W('Locktown Baptist Church')]),
N('old-stone-church-kingwood','Old Stone Church, Kingwood','Kingwood Township','njc','worship','standing',40.54278,-75.00556,'exact','view','drive','','1837',
  'A sandstone Presbyterian church of 1837, built probably from the stones of a smaller meeting house of 1755 on the same ground; George Whitefield preached to the congregation in 1739. The one-room Oak Summit School beside it was used from 1849 to 1953, and the old cemetery lies across Oak Summit Road.',
  'The stone church, the schoolhouse next door and the 1754 cemetery across the road.',
  '',
  'Owned by the First Unitarian Universalist Fellowship of Hunterdon County, a working congregation: view from Oak Summit Road and ask before walking the grounds. No parking is mapped.',
  [W('Old Stone Church (Kingwood Township, New Jersey)')]),
]
